: \nL167: 【72†Learn about vigilant mode†docs.github.com】.\nL168: # gnark v0.16.4\nL169: \nL170: This release contains a security fix for a high-severity soundness issue in emulated elliptic-curve scalar multiplication, which made ECDSA signature forgery possible. It also has another soundness fix in the BW6-761 pairing gadget, several correctness fixes in pairing and recursion gadgets, new in-circuit performance work, a public GKR prover/verifier API and an experimental WebGPU+WASM proving backend.\nL171: > We strongly recommend that all users upgrade to v0.16.4.\nL172: > Many circuits have new constraint systems in this release. Read Breaking changes below before upgrading. You will probably need to recompile circuits, run a new setup and redeploy verifiers.\nL173: \nL174: * * *\nL175: ## ⚠️ Security\nL176: ### 【73†GHSA-7fx8-hmgc-82jp】 — ECDSA / P256Verify signature forgery in emulated fake-GLV scalar multiplication (High)\nL177: \nL178: Advisory: 【73†GHSA-7fx8-hmgc-82jp】\nL179: Affected: `< v0.16.4` · Patched: `v0.16.4`\nL180: `sw_emulated` scalar multiplication with fake-GLV (`scalarMulFakeGLV`, `scalarMulGLVAndFakeGLV`) gets the result point from a prover-supplied hint and then checks a relation, without computing the point in-circuit. Nothing checked that the hinted point is on the curve. The final accumulator identity is a group-law argument, so it holds trivially for an off-curve operand. A malicious prover could therefore make the gadget return any point they chose.\nL181:   * P-256, P-384, STARK curve: full ECDSA signature forgery. With `s = -1` and a degenerate decomposition, the accumulator collapses to the point at infinity at every step, so the check passes for any `R.x`. This affects `std/signature/ecdsa` and any circuit using `ScalarMul`, `JointScalarMulBase` or `MultiScalarMul` on these curves, including the EIP-7951 `P256Verify` precompile gadget.\nL182:   * secp256k1, BN254 (emulated): same missing check. These curves have cofactor 1, so the subgroup check is a no-op and nothing else constrained the hint. This affects the `ECRECOVER` (0x01) and BN254 `ECMUL` (0x07) precompile gadgets.\nL183:   * BLS12-381, BW6-761 (emulated): not affected. They use classic GLV, which computes the result in-circuit.\nL184: The fix adds an on-curve check on the hinted output, at about 325 R1CS constraints per hinted scalar multiplication (~0.2%).\nL185: The same fix also corrects `AddUnified` on curves with `a ≠ 0` (j ≠ 0, e.g. P-256, P-384, STARK curve). When `y1 + y2 = 0` but `x1 ≠ x2`, the old code returned the point at infinity instead of the correct sum. This could happen with two valid on-curve points and an honest witness. `AddUnified` now uses the same chord/tangent selection as the j = 0 path. It costs about 6.3k R1CS constraints on P-256 ECDSA (~4.4%); j = 0 curves are not affected.\nL186: `emulated.Field.IsZero` is also hardened for inputs that reduce to canonical zero.\nL187: The `ScalarMul`, `JointScalarMulBase` and `MultiScalarMul` docs now state the guarantee: the returned point is on the curve and in the prime-order subgroup on every supported curve. Callers do not need to check this again.\nL188: ### BW6-761 `AssertFinalExponentiationIsOne` accepted a zero accumulator (【74†#1871】)\nL189: The emulated BW6-761 `Pairing.AssertFinalExponentiationIsOne` did not require the hinted residue witness to be non-zero. With a zero Miller-loop accumulator, an all-zero witness made the check reduce to `0 == 0`, so the gadget accepted a value outside the target group. This is the same class of bug as 【75†GHSA-3mvx-pp85-pm65】, which was fixed in v0.16.0 for BN254, BLS12-381 and BLS12-377 but missed for BW6-761. `PairingCheck` is not affected.\nL190: ### Documented caller obligations\nL191: \nL192: These are documentation-only changes, but they describe assumptions that callers must meet:\nL193:   * EIP-4844 KZG point evaluation (`std/evmprecompiles.KzgPointEvaluation*`): the caller must range-check every input limb to 128 bits (16 bits for the `*16` variants). The gadget does not check this, so unchecked limbs let several public inputs map to the same precompile input (【76†#1863】).\nL194:   * Precomputed G2 line evaluations (`G2Affine.Lines`, `NewG2AffineFixed*`): when lines are supplied, `Pair` and `MillerLoop` use them as given and do not check that Q is in the subgroup. Only the in-circuit line-computation path (`Lines == nil`) performs that check (【77†#1858】).\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInternal Error ()\nciteturn3view1 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://github.com/THU-HAS/MegaZK/blob/main/LICENSE\",\"lineno\":null}); \nFailed to fetch https://github.com/THU-HAS/MegaZK/blob/main/LICENSE: Cache miss--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInternal Error ()\nciteturn3view2 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://github.com/Consensys-Incorporated/gnark/blob/master/LICENSE\",\"lineno\":null}); \nFailed to fetch https://github.com/Consensys-Incorporated/gnark/blob/master/LICENSE: Cache miss--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n6.1. CUPTI Activity API — Cupti (https://docs.nvidia.com/cupti/13.2.1/api/group__CUPTI__ACTIVITY__API.html)\nciteturn3view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://docs.nvidia.com/cupti/13.2.1/api/group__CUPTI__ACTIVITY__API.html\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 7634\nL0: Back to top\nL1: \nL2: [Input: Search the docs ...] `Ctrl`+`K`\nL3: \nL4: Table of Contents\nL5:   * 【0†1. Release Notes】\nL6:   * 【1†2. Usage】\nL7:   * 【2†3. Library support】\nL8:   * 【3†4. Special Configurations】\nL9:   * 【4†5. Tutorial】\nL10:   * 【5†6. Modules】\nL11:     * 【6†6.1. CUPTI Activity API】\nL12:     * 【7†6.2. CUPTI Callback API】\nL13:     * 【8†6.3. CUPTI Checkpoint API】\nL14:     * 【9†6.4. CUPTI PC Sampling API】\nL15:     * 【10†6.5. CUPTI PC Sampling Utility API】